One common way the Java API can be misused is through improper handling of user input when interacting with the system shell. This can lead to Command Injection vulnerabilities.

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;

public class CommandInjectionDemo {
    public static void pingHost(String host) throws Exception {
        // This code is vulnerable to command injection because it concatenates 
        // untrusted user input directly into a system command string.
        // An attacker could provide input like "8.8.8.8; rm -rf /"
        String command = "ping -c 3 " + host;

        Process process = Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command);

        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Simulating malicious user input
        pingHost("8.8.8.8; cat /etc/passwd");
    }
}
```

